A large cdimage medium is a DVD-ROM, not an overlong CD (doc 17, M5g).

A folder in the drive (isodir:) can hold more than a CD does, and past an
80-minute CD -- CD_MAX_SECTORS, the line QEMU's own media_is_dvd() already
draws -- calling it a CD is a lie the guest can catch: MSF runs out at
99:59:74, so a TOC lead-out, a subchannel position and a raw sector header
have no address for anything beyond, and READ DVD STRUCTURE (which needs no
patch: cmd_read_dvd_structure refuses only media_is_cd) answers for a disc
the drive says is a CD.

So the disc model's GET CONFIGURATION now reports the profile the medium
actually is -- DVD-ROM current with CD-ROM also listed, or CD-ROM alone --
and adds the DVD Read feature (0x001f) beside CD Read and CD Audio, which a
DVD-ROM drive has and a CD-ROM drive does not. Mode page 2A's byte 10 gains
the DVD-ROM read bit in the same condition, because the two answers have to
agree or cdrom.sys believes neither.

MMC has the drive decide its feature list and only the current profile
follow the medium. Ours holds exactly one medium at a time and a guest
re-reads both of these on every media change, so letting the capability
follow the disc costs nothing and keeps every CD machine's answers byte for
byte what they were: with a CD in the tray this emits exactly the bytes it
emitted before, profile list included.

The profile list is the one feature whose length depends on the medium (one
profile or two), so it moves out of the fixed-body table and is written by
hand; both of the RT selection rules reduce to "only when the request starts
at feature 0", since 0 is the lowest feature code there is.

GET CONFIGURATION also answers through the traced reply path now: which
profile the drive claimed for the medium in it is exactly what a
CDIMAGE_TRACE=1 run wants to read back, and it was the one answer in the
disc model that printed its packet and not its reply.

That is also how the one flaw here turned up: the reply is built whole and
truncated to the allocation length at the end, so a short GET CONFIGURATION
left stale io_buffer bytes inside the profile list. They reached no guest,
and now reach no log either -- the memset covers everything the function may
write, not only what the guest asked for.

diff --git a/hw/ide/atapi.c b/hw/ide/atapi.c
index c0cef9c65b..97adb48268 100644
--- a/hw/ide/atapi.c
+++ b/hw/ide/atapi.c
@@ -1284,11 +1284,18 @@ static void cmd_inquiry(IDEState *s, uint8_t *buf)
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_CDIMAGE
 /*
- * GET CONFIGURATION with a disc model: a CD-ROM drive (one profile), the CD
- * read feature with C2 pointers and CD external audio play. RT (byte 1 & 3)
+ * GET CONFIGURATION with a disc model: a CD-ROM drive with a CD in it, a
+ * DVD-ROM drive with a DVD in it (media_is_dvd: anything past an 80-minute
+ * CD, which is where a generated folder disc becomes one, M5g), the CD read
+ * feature with C2 pointers and CD external audio play. RT (byte 1 & 3)
  * 0 = every feature from the starting one on, 1 = the current ones, 2 = that
  * feature alone; an unsupported starting feature yields the header only,
  * as drives answer cdrom.sys' per-feature probes.
+ *
+ * MMC has the *drive* decide its feature list, with only the current profile
+ * following the medium. Ours holds exactly one medium and a guest re-reads
+ * this on every media change, so letting the capability follow the disc keeps
+ * every CD machine's answers byte for byte what they were.
  */
 static void atapi_disc_get_configuration(IDEState *s, uint8_t *buf)
 {
@@ -1297,10 +1304,11 @@ static void atapi_disc_get_configuration(IDEState *s, uint8_t *buf)
         uint8_t version_flags;
         uint8_t body[4];
     } features[] = {
-        { 0x0000, (0 << 2) | 0x03, { 0x00, 0x08, 0x01, 0x00 } }, /* profile list: CD-ROM, current */
         { 0x001e, (2 << 2) | 0x03, { 0x02, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00 } }, /* CD read: C2 error pointers */
+        { 0x001f, (0 << 2) | 0x03, { 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00 } }, /* DVD read (DVD only) */
         { 0x0103, (1 << 2) | 0x03, { 0x07, 0x00, 0x01, 0x00 } }, /* CD audio: scan, mute, volume, 256 levels */
     };
+    bool dvd = media_is_dvd(s);
     int rt = buf[1] & 3;
     int start = lduw_be_p(buf + 2);
     int max_len = lduw_be_p(buf + 7);
@@ -1313,9 +1321,37 @@ static void atapi_disc_get_configuration(IDEState *s, uint8_t *buf)
     if (max_len > BDRV_SECTOR_SIZE) {
         max_len = BDRV_SECTOR_SIZE;
     }
-    memset(buf, 0, MAX(max_len, 8));
-    stw_be_p(buf + 6, MMC_PROFILE_CD_ROM);
+    /*
+     * The whole reply is built and only then truncated to max_len, so clear
+     * everything this may write rather than only what the guest asked for:
+     * the bytes past a short allocation length reach no guest but do reach
+     * the trace, where stale io_buffer reads as a drive answering nonsense.
+     */
+    memset(buf, 0, MAX(max_len, 8 + 12 + (int)ARRAY_SIZE(features) * 8));
+    stw_be_p(buf + 6, dvd ? MMC_PROFILE_DVD_ROM : MMC_PROFILE_CD_ROM);
+    /*
+     * Feature 0x0000, the profile list, is the one whose length depends on
+     * the medium: the disc's own profile first and current, the other one
+     * listed and not. Both selection rules reduce to "only when the request
+     * starts at feature 0", since 0 is the lowest code there is.
+     */
+    if (start == 0) {
+        int n = dvd ? 8 : 4;
+
+        stw_be_p(buf + len, 0x0000);
+        buf[len + 2] = (0 << 2) | 0x03;
+        buf[len + 3] = n;
+        stw_be_p(buf + len + 4, dvd ? MMC_PROFILE_DVD_ROM : MMC_PROFILE_CD_ROM);
+        buf[len + 6] = 1; /* current */
+        if (dvd) {
+            stw_be_p(buf + len + 8, MMC_PROFILE_CD_ROM);
+        }
+        len += 4 + n;
+    }
     for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(features); i++) {
+        if (features[i].code == 0x001f && !dvd) {
+            continue;
+        }
         if (rt == 2 ? features[i].code != start : features[i].code < start) {
             continue;
         }
@@ -1329,7 +1365,9 @@ static void atapi_disc_get_configuration(IDEState *s, uint8_t *buf)
         len += 8;
     }
     stl_be_p(buf, len - 4);
-    ide_atapi_cmd_reply(s, len, max_len);
+    /* through the traced reply: which profile the drive claimed for the
+       medium in it is exactly the thing a CDIMAGE_TRACE run wants to see */
+    atapi_disc_reply(s, len, max_len);
 }
 #endif
 
@@ -1485,7 +1523,11 @@ static void cmd_mode_sense(IDEState *s, uint8_t *buf)
 #ifdef CONFIG_CDIMAGE
             if (atapi_disc(s)) {
                 /* a CD-ROM drive of the period, 48x, full CD-DA / subchannel */
-                buf[10] = 0x03; /* reads CD-R and CD-RW, no DVD */
+                buf[10] = media_is_dvd(s) ? 0x0b : 0x03; /* CD-R, CD-RW, and
+                                   DVD-ROM read while a DVD is in the tray —
+                                   the profile in GET CONFIGURATION and this
+                                   bit have to agree or cdrom.sys believes
+                                   neither */
                 buf[13] = 0x7f; /* CD-DA commands, stream accurate, R-W raw and
                                    corrected, C2 pointers, ISRC, UPC */
                 buf[15] = 0x03; /* separate volume and mute */
